{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Matters of dispute</b> — that the sages of the city are divided over the matter: this one declares impure and this one declares pure, this one holds liable and this one holds exempt — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 17:8), and it is the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 17:8). But it is not correct. Rather, according to the midrash (Sifrei Shofetim 152): \"between blood and blood\" — between the blood of a menstruant and the blood of a woman who gave birth and the blood of a zavah; \"between law and law\" — between monetary law and capital cases and laws of lashes; \"between plague-mark and plague-mark\" — between plagues of a person and plagues of houses and plagues of garments; \"matters of dispute\" — the water of the sotah and the breaking of the heifer's neck and the purification of the metzora.\n\nNow, the reason for וּבֵין (and between) is, according to them, that the לַמֶּ\"ד serves as a prefix like בֵּי\"ת: בֵּין דָּם בְּדָם דִּין בְּדִין — meaning that a matter arises for judgment concerning one of all the bloods and concerning one of all the laws. And similarly לוּז שֵׁם הָעִיר לָרִאשֹׁנָה (Genesis 28:19) — לָרִאשֹׁנָה means בָּרִאשׁוֹנָה; נְגָפוֹ יהוה בְּמֵעָיו לְחֳלִי לְאֵין מַרְפֵּא (II Chronicles 21:18) — בְּחֹלִי בְּאֵין מַרְפֵּא; וַיְחַפֵּהוּ זָהָב טוֹב לְכִכָּרִים שֵׁשׁ מֵאוֹת (ibid. 3:8) — בְּכִכָּרִים. And so there are many such instances. Or its reason is: \"between blood\" — to all the bloods; \"between law\" — to all the laws. And similarly: וְכָל רִיב אֲשֶׁר יָבֹא עֲלֵיכֶם מֵאֲחֵיכֶם וגוֹ', בֵּין דָּם לְדָם, בֵּין תּוֹרָה לְמִצְוָה לְחֻקִּים וּלְמִשְׁפָּטִים, וְהִזְהַרְתָּם אֹתָם וְלֹא יֶאְשְׁמוּ לַיְיָ (ibid. 19:10).\n\nAccording to its plain sense: בֵּין דָּם לְדָם וגוֹ' — between matters of homicide or monetary law or wound and bruise; כָּל דִּבְרֵי רִיבֹת אֲשֶׁר יִהְיוּ בִּשְׁעָרֶיךָ — all matters of dispute that may arise in your gates. And the verse speaks of what commonly occurs, for in matters of judgments disputes will be more frequent than in other rulings. But the law of the elder applies equally to all of them, and therefore it says (Deuteronomy 17:11): וְעָשִׂיתָ עַל פִּי הַתּוֹרָה וגוֹ' וְעַל הַמִּשְׁפָּט וגוֹ' — for <b>the Torah</b> alludes to all other rulings."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The abbreviation דהי\"ב is resolved as II Chronicles; וגוֹ' rendered as 'etc.' throughout; the masculine verb forms in וְהִזְהַרְתֶּם and similar are retained as quoted from the biblical Hebrew, not normalized."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:48.170834898Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}